# E. Correct the model before calling it the final HPOP run

The current implementation exercise revealed several mathematical and modelling problems. These should be addressed before the final Run 2.

## 1. Remove the free per-segment penalty

Run 1 uses:

[
\lambda_{\mathrm{seg}}>0.
]

For the generative semi-Markov model, replace this with:

* normalized duration distributions;
* normalized transition probabilities;
* start and terminal transitions;
* fixed-length normalization.

Do not use an unnormalized factor:

[
\exp(-\lambda_{\mathrm{seg}})
]

when comparing marginal likelihood across (K).

Your report already notes that the manuscript’s unnormalized segmentation prior invalidates raw likelihood comparison and that the implementation had to compute the corrected conditional likelihood. 

## 2. Add a composition emission

At present, the recurrent score mostly captures order. But skill identity also depends on **which CPA types normally belong to the skill**.

Use a segment likelihood such as:

[
\log e_{n,k}(a,b)
=================

\lambda_{\mathrm{comp}}
\log p_{\mathrm{comp}}
\left(
x^{(n)}*{a:b}\mid k
\right)
+
\lambda*{\mathrm{po}}
\log p_{\mathrm{RFS}}
\left(
x^{(n)}_{a:b}\mid H_k
\right).
]

This gives:

* the composition term: “does this block contain the right kinds of actions?”;
* the partial-order term: “are their dependencies and repetitions plausible?”.

The HSMM already reaches approximately (0.80) synthetic ARI using composition alone, which shows that composition is doing important identification work. 

## 3. Restore an observation-noise floor

A precedence violation should not automatically receive probability zero.

Use a trembling-hand mixture such as:

[
p_{\mathrm{step}}(c_t)
======================

(1-\epsilon)
p_{\mathrm{RFS}}(c_t)
+
\epsilon p_{\mathrm{bg}}(c_t),
\qquad
\epsilon>0.
]

This gives finite held-out likelihood under occasional violations or annotation errors.

## 4. Correct the repair-trigger mechanism

The most important structural defect is that current invalidation only flows forward along the inferred precedence relation.

That means:

[
\textsc{failed test}
]

cannot invalidate the earlier:

[
\textsc{edit}
]

that needs to be revised. The model can explain recomputation after returning to the edit, but it does not explain what caused the return.

In Run 2, condition repair activation on observable outcomes. For example:

[
J_{\mathrm{repair}}
\left(
\textsc{Run-Test},
\textsc{Edit};
o_t
\right)
]

can be positive when:

[
o_t=\textsc{failure}.
]

A simple first implementation can make the repeat/back-jump cost outcome-dependent:

[
\lambda_{\mathrm{rep}}(x,o_t)
]

so revisiting an edit after a failing test is less penalized than repeating an already-valid action without a failure signal.

## 5. Use exact semi-Markov E-steps

The report correctly observes that the amortized inference network is unnecessary when the generative model and posterior use the same semi-Markov lattice.

Use:

[
q(S_n)
======

p_\Theta
\left(
S_n\mid \mathbf c^{(n)},\widetilde B_n
\right)
]

computed directly by forward–backward.

This removes:

* inference-network hyperparameters;
* amortization error;
* a mismatch between generative and inference scores.

## 6. Treat the sequential component as a separate model

The real-data bigram advantage indicates that partial-order structure and local sequential prediction capture different information.

Add this as a separate model:

[
\text{HPOP+Seq}.
]

For example:

[
W_t(x)
======

F_t^{\mathrm{RFS}}(x)
\exp
\left{
\beta Q_t(x)
+
\eta\log P_k(x\mid y_{t-1})
\right}.
]

The sequential term is evaluated only among frontier-feasible or nearly feasible roles.

Compare:

* HSMM;
* HPOP;
* HPOP+Seq;
* standalone bigram.

Do not silently add the sequential term to HPOP and remove the original ablation. You need to show whether it closes the predictive gap while retaining partial-order behavior.

